  "name": "LEPTOSPIRA - IgM RAPID CARD",
  "code": "2801",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">COMMENTS:</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">Leptospirosis, a potentially deadly zoonosis, is caused by the spirochete leptospira. An extensive range of symptoms are known: fever, </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">headaches, vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhea, rashes, jaundice or red eyes. The course of the disease can become considerably </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">dangerous if not treated at the earliest, kidney damage, meningitis, liver failure and respiratory problems have also been reported. Thus </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">early diagnosis of the disease is of utmost importance. Leptospira IgG/IgM rapid test is a qualitative test for the detection of genus specific </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">human antibodies against leptospira in serum or plasma. The IgM asaay is recommended for the detection of acute leprospirosis, whereas </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">the IgG test is a rational application in addition to the IgM test and completes the diagnostic results.</span><br><strong><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">LIMITATIONS:</span></strong><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">-A negative result for an individual subject indicates absence of detectable L.interrogans antibodies but it does not preclude the possibility </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">of exposure to L.interrogans. </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">-A negative result can occur if the quantity of L.interrogans antibodies present in the specimen is below the detection limit of the assay or</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">the antibodies that are detected are not present during the stage of disease in which a sample is collected. </span><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">-Some specimens containing high titres of heterophile antibodies or rheumatoid factor may affect expected results.</span></p>",
